**Answer: Yes.** On a convex (hence simply connected) region, every nonvanishing analytic function admits an analytic logarithm.

**Proof.**

Since $A$ is convex, it is in particular simply connected (every closed curve in $A$ can be contracted to a point through the convex hull, which stays in $A$). Since $f(z) \neq 0$ for all $z \in A$, the quotient

$$\frac{f'(z)}{f(z)}$$

is analytic on all of $A$.

Fix a base point $z_0 \in A$ and define, for any $z \in A$,

$$F(z) \;=\; \int_{z_0}^{z} \frac{f'(\zeta)}{f(\zeta)}\,d\zeta,$$

where the integral is taken along the straight line segment $[z_0, z]$, which lies entirely in $A$ by convexity. Because $A$ is simply connected and $f'/f$ is analytic on $A$, Cauchy's theorem guarantees that this integral is independent of the path chosen (any two paths from $z_0$ to $z$ form a closed curve, over which the integral vanishes). Hence $F$ is a well-defined analytic function on $A$, and by the fundamental theorem of calculus for analytic functions,

$$F'(z) \;=\; \frac{f'(z)}{f(z)}.$$

Now consider the function

$$h(z) \;=\; f(z)\,e^{-F(z)}.$$

Differentiating,

$$h'(z) \;=\; f'(z)\,e^{-F(z)} \;-\; f(z)\,F'(z)\,e^{-F(z)} \;=\; \Bigl(f'(z) - f(z)\cdot\frac{f'(z)}{f(z)}\Bigr)e^{-F(z)} \;=\; 0.$$

Since $A$ is connected (being convex), $h$ is constant: $h(z) = c$ for some constant $c \in \mathbb{C}$. Because $f$ never vanishes and $e^{-F}$ never vanishes, $c \neq 0$. Therefore we may write $c = e^{w_0}$ for some $w_0 \in \mathbb{C}$ (every nonzero complex number is the exponential of some complex number). It follows that

$$f(z) \;=\; c\,e^{F(z)} \;=\; e^{F(z) + w_0}.$$

Setting $g(z) = F(z) + w_0$ gives an analytic function on $A$ satisfying

$$f(z) = e^{g(z)} \quad \text{for all } z \in A. \qquad \blacksquare$$

**Remark on the role of convexity.** The only topological fact used is that $A$ is simply connected, which ensures $f'/f$ has a single-valued antiderivative. Convexity is a sufficient (and stronger) condition guaranteeing simple connectivity. On a non-simply-connected domain the statement can fail: for example, $f(z) = z$ on $A = \mathbb{C}\setminus\{0\}$ is nonvanishing and analytic, but admits no analytic logarithm (the winding number obstruction prevents $\log z$ from being single-valued).

